({[({(A larger ratio is **worse**.)}])}

Here's the reasoning: your ratio is

$$R = \frac{\text{repetition} \mid \text{ground truth prefix}}{\text{repetition} \mid \text{model's own prefix}}$$

Since the goal is to **minimize repetition**, a larger ratio tells you that the numerator (repetition under the ground-truth prefix) is growing relative to the denominator. That means the model is falling into repetitive loops *more* when it is anchored to the correct prefix, which is the exact outcome you are trying to avoid.

A smaller ratio (approaching 0 or 1 from below) would indicate that the model is not significantly more repetitive in the ground-truth condition than in the self-prefix condition—i.e., repetition is being kept in check.

({[({(In short: minimize the ratio, because a high value signals elevated repetition in the ground-truth setting.)}])}
